@node Player state accessors
@section Player state accessors

@findex spofy-player-current-track
@findex spofy-player-playing-p
@findex spofy-player-current-track-id

The function @code{spofy-player-current-track} returns the current track as a cons cell @code{(NAME . ARTIST)}, or @code{nil} if nothing is playing.  This is useful in custom mode-line constructs or hook functions.

The function @code{spofy-player-playing-p} returns non-nil if Spotify is currently playing.

The function @code{spofy-player-current-track-id} returns the Spotify ID of the currently playing track, or @code{nil}.  This is used internally by search and browse buffers to highlight the now-playing row.

@node Entity access in buffers
@section Entity access in buffers

@findex spofy-browse-entity-at-point
@findex spofy-library-entity-at-point

The function @code{spofy-browse-entity-at-point} returns the full Spotify API alist for the entity on the current @code{tabulated-list} row in any browse buffer (@ref{Browsing}).  This is useful for writing custom actions or hook functions that need to access entity metadata.

The function @code{spofy-library-entity-at-point} does the same for library buffers (@ref{Library management}).

@node API client
@section API client

@findex spofy-api-get
@findex spofy-api-put
@findex spofy-api-post
@findex spofy-api-delete

The API client in @samp{spofy-api.el} provides four high-level functions for interacting with the Spotify Web API:

@itemize
@item
@code{spofy-api-get} sends a GET request to an endpoint with optional query parameters.
@item
@code{spofy-api-put} sends a PUT request with an optional JSON body.
@item
@code{spofy-api-post} sends a POST request with an optional JSON body.
@item
@code{spofy-api-delete} sends a DELETE request with an optional JSON body.
@end itemize

All four accept an endpoint path (relative to @samp{https://api.spotify.com/v1/}), optional data, and a callback function that receives the parsed JSON response.  They handle authentication headers, token refresh, rate limiting (429 responses), and transient error retry automatically.

These functions are the building blocks for all of Spofy's features.  Advanced users who want to extend Spofy with custom API calls can use them directly.

@node Authentication helpers
@section Authentication helpers

@findex spofy-auth-access-token
@findex spofy-auth-refresh-token

The function @code{spofy-auth-access-token} returns the current OAuth2 access token, or @code{nil} if unavailable or expired.  If the token has expired and a refresh token is available, it automatically attempts a refresh before returning @code{nil}.

The function @code{spofy-auth-refresh-token} explicitly uses the stored refresh token to obtain a new access token from Spotify.  It requires @code{spofy-client-id} and @code{spofy-client-secret} to be set, and signals an error if either is missing.  This is called automatically when needed, but can be invoked manually if you encounter authentication issues.

@node Formatting utilities
@section Formatting utilities

@findex spofy-ui-format-duration-ms
@findex spofy-ui-format-artists
@findex spofy-ui-truncate

The function @code{spofy-ui-format-duration-ms} takes a duration in milliseconds and returns a human-readable @code{"M:SS"} string.

The function @code{spofy-ui-format-artists} takes a list of artist alists (as returned by the Spotify API) and formats them as a comma-separated string of names.

The function @code{spofy-ui-truncate} truncates a string to a given maximum display width.  It accepts an optional FACE argument; when provided, the face is applied to the result and, if truncation occurred, the last three characters are marked with the @code{spofy-fade} text property (levels 1, 2, 3).  After @code{tabulated-list-print}, the advice function @code{spofy-ui--after-tabulated-list-print} calls @code{spofy-ui--apply-buffer-fades}, which creates overlays on the marked characters with foreground colors blended toward the default background at 25%, 50%, and 75% respectively.  This produces a smooth fade-out effect instead of a hard cut or an explicit ellipsis character.  Because the color blending happens at display time via overlays rather than being baked into text properties, the fade automatically adapts when the user switches themes: @code{spofy-ui--refresh-fades} is registered on @code{enable-theme-functions} and recomputes all fade overlays in Spofy buffers.  It uses @code{truncate-string-to-width} internally, so column alignment is correct even with variable-width characters.  All entry formatting functions in the browse, library, search, consult, and playlist modules pass the appropriate face to @code{spofy-ui-truncate}, which handles both truncation and propertizing in one call.  The mode-line and tab-bar modules call it without a face, so truncation there is silent.

@node Consult integration
@chapter Consult integration

@findex consult-spofy-track
@findex consult-spofy-album
@findex consult-spofy-artist
@findex consult-spofy-playlist
@findex spofy-library-search-playlists
@findex consult-spofy-device

The file @samp{spofy-consult.el} provides six @code{consult} completion sources for searching and selecting Spotify entities from the minibuffer.  These require the @samp{consult} package.

All candidates are displayed in aligned tabular columns with faces matching the search result buffers:

The command @code{consult-spofy-track} lets you search for tracks with live narrowing as you type (debounced at 300ms to avoid excessive API calls).  Candidates show Name, Artist(s), Album, and Duration columns.  Selecting a track plays it immediately.

The command @code{consult-spofy-album} provides the same experience for albums, with Name, Artist(s), Year, and track count columns.  Selecting an album opens the album view (@ref{Album view}).

The command @code{consult-spofy-artist} searches artists, with Name, Genres, and Followers columns.  Selecting an artist opens the artist view (@ref{Artist view}).

The command @code{consult-spofy-playlist} searches all Spotify playlists with live narrowing, with Name, Owner, and track count columns.  Selecting a playlist opens the playlist detail view (@ref{Playlist detail view}).

The command @code{spofy-library-search-playlists} fetches the user's own playlists with incremental narrowing, with Name, Owner, and track count columns.  The full list is fetched once from the API and filtered client-side as you type.  Selecting a playlist opens the playlist detail view.

The command @code{consult-spofy-device} fetches available playback devices with incremental narrowing, with Name and Type columns.  Selecting a device transfers playback to it.

All six commands use @code{consult--dynamic-collection} for incremental search with synchronous API requests (via @code{url-retrieve-synchronously}), as required by consult's dynamic collection contract.  Each candidate stores the full Spotify entity in a text property, making them compatible with the embark integration (@ref{Embark integration}).

@node Embark integration
@chapter Embark integration

@findex spofy-embark-copy-uri

The file @samp{spofy-embark.el} registers four entity categories with @samp{embark}, each with a keymap of contextual actions.  These actions work on Spotify entities selected through @code{consult} (@ref{Consult integration}) or in @code{tabulated-list} buffers.

The @strong{spofy-track} category provides actions to play the track, add it to a playlist, save it to the library, view its album, view its artist, or copy its Spotify URI@.

The @strong{spofy-album} category provides actions to play the album, save it, view its tracks, view its artist, or copy its URI@.

The @strong{spofy-artist} category provides actions to play the artist's top tracks, view the artist's albums, or copy the URI@.

The @strong{spofy-playlist} category provides actions to play the playlist, view its tracks, follow it, unfollow it, or copy its URI@.

The command @code{spofy-embark-copy-uri} copies the Spotify URI of the target entity to the kill ring.  This is available in all four category keymaps and is useful for sharing links or constructing org-mode links manually.

@node Org-mode integration
@chapter Org-mode integration

@findex spofy-org-follow
@findex spofy-org-store
@findex spofy-org-export

The file @samp{spofy-org.el} registers a @samp{spotify:} link type with Org mode, supporting three operations.

The function @code{spofy-org-follow} handles opening @code{spotify:} links.  It parses the link path to determine the entity type and ID, then dispatches to the appropriate Spofy command: tracks are played, while albums, artists, and playlists open their respective browse views (@ref{Browsing}).  This means you can write @samp{[[spotify:track:4uLU6hMCjMI75M1A2tKUQC][Never Gonna Give You Up]]} in an Org file and open it to play the track.

The function @code{spofy-org-store} enables @samp{org-store-link} in any Spofy buffer.  It extracts the URI and a descriptive label from the entity at point and stores it for later insertion with @samp{org-insert-link}.

The function @code{spofy-org-export} generates appropriate links for export backends.  In HTML, @LaTeX{}, and Markdown, it produces links to @samp{open.spotify.com/TYPE/ID} so readers can open the content in their browser or Spotify app.

@node Faces
@chapter Faces

@vindex spofy-track-name
@vindex spofy-artist-name
@vindex spofy-album-name
@vindex spofy-playing
@vindex spofy-playing-icon
@vindex spofy-header
@vindex spofy-muted

Spofy defines a set of faces in the @code{spofy-faces} customization group for controlling the appearance of its buffers:

@itemize
@item
@code{spofy-track-name} is used for track names in all listings.  It inherits from @code{font-lock-keyword-face}.
@item
@code{spofy-artist-name} is used for artist names.  It inherits from @code{font-lock-function-name-face}.
@item
@code{spofy-album-name} is used for album names.  It inherits from @code{font-lock-type-face}.
@item
@code{spofy-playing} marks the currently playing track in any listing.  It uses bold weight and the Spotify green color (@samp{#1DB954}).
@item
@code{spofy-playing-icon} is used for the ▶ icon on the currently playing track.  It inherits from @code{spofy-playing}.
@item
@code{spofy-header} is used for the header area in album, artist, and playlist detail views.  It inherits from @code{header-line}.
@item
@code{spofy-muted} is used for secondary text like durations and dates.  It inherits from @code{shadow}.
@end itemize

All faces can be customized with @samp{M-x customize-group RET spofy-faces RET}.

@node Hooks
@chapter Hooks

@vindex spofy-player-state-changed-hook
@vindex spofy-player-track-changed-hook
@vindex spofy-authenticated-hook

The hook @code{spofy-player-state-changed-hook} runs whenever any aspect of the player state changes: track, play/pause state, shuffle, repeat, volume, or device.  The mode-line display and dashboard now-playing section both use this hook to stay up to date.  Add your own functions to this hook if you want to react to any state change.

The hook @code{spofy-player-track-changed-hook} runs only when the current track changes.  This is a subset of state changes and is useful for notifications or logging.  The desktop notification feature (@ref{Playback, , Playback options}) hooks into this.

The hook @code{spofy-authenticated-hook} runs once after a successful OAuth2 authentication.  Use it to perform setup that depends on having a valid token, such as pre-fetching the user's playlists.

@node Mode-line display
@chapter Mode-line display

@findex spofy-mode-line-mode

The command @code{spofy-mode-line-mode} toggles a global minor mode that adds a Spofy segment to the mode line.  The segment shows the currently playing track formatted according to @code{spofy-mode-line-format} (@ref{Mode-line, , Mode-line options}).

When enabled, the mode hooks into @code{spofy-player-state-changed-hook} to rebuild the mode-line string whenever the player state changes, then calls @code{force-mode-line-update} to refresh all frames.

The mode is enabled automatically when @code{spofy-global-mode} is active and @code{spofy-enable-mode-line} is non-nil (@ref{Global key and mode-line activation, , Global options}).

@node Tab-bar display
@chapter Tab-bar display

@findex spofy-tab-bar-mode

The command @code{spofy-tab-bar-mode} toggles a global minor mode that adds a Spofy segment to the tab bar.  The segment shows the currently playing track formatted according to @code{spofy-tab-bar-format} (@ref{Tab-bar, , Tab-bar options}).

When enabled, the mode appends a @code{tab-bar-format-spofy} entry to @code{tab-bar-format} and hooks into @code{spofy-player-state-changed-hook} to rebuild the tab-bar string whenever the player state changes.

The mode is enabled automatically when @code{spofy-global-mode} is active and @code{spofy-enable-tab-bar} is non-nil (@ref{Global key and mode-line activation, , Global options}).

